# PART V — HOW WE MEASURE PROGRESS

## 13. Capability Evaluation Matrix

Every integrated model version must have a capability matrix.

| Capability | Required evidence |
|---|---|
| Reasoning | Unseen multi-step problems |
| Mathematics | Held-out mathematical tasks |
| Programming | Real code generation and repair |
| Language | Diverse natural-language tasks |
| Generalization | New task families |
| Learning | Improvement after new information |
| Memory | Delayed recall and retrieval accuracy |
| Long context | Position-sensitive recall and reasoning |
| Agents | End-to-end task completion |
| Computer control | Successful verified operations |
| Tool use | Correct selection and execution |
| Reliability | Error and false-acceptance rates |
| Efficiency | CPU time, RAM and energy |

No single benchmark can establish general intelligence.

## 14. Efficiency Evaluation Matrix

Every model version must record:

- Parameter count.
- Model file size.
- Peak RAM.
- Average and p95 latency.
- Tokens per second.
- CPU utilization.
- Energy per task.
- Task completion time.
- Accuracy at each resource budget.

We will compare models at matched hardware and clearly defined workloads.

## 15. Competitive Target

The aspiration is to compete with leading systems from companies such as Anthropic and Google and other frontier AI developers.

The comparison set must be verified at the time of evaluation because model names, versions and availability change.

The ambition of tenfold superiority must be converted into separate quantitative targets.

For example:

- Tenfold lower memory on a defined workload.
- Tenfold lower energy per successful task.
- Tenfold faster completion of a defined task.
- Better task accuracy under a matched inference budget.

These are separate achievements. One does not establish the others.

The project must never claim tenfold overall intelligence from one benchmark.

