# PART IX — DECISION GATES AND STOP CONDITIONS

## 29. When to Continue

Continue a mechanism when:

- It produces reproducible improvements.
- It generalizes beyond its development data.
- It has acceptable resource cost.
- It does not introduce unacceptable regressions.
- Its benefits survive independent testing.

## 30. When to Redesign

Redesign a component when:

- Its core hypothesis repeatedly fails.
- It cannot generalize outside narrow benchmarks.
- It introduces unacceptable computational cost.
- It conflicts with other essential mechanisms.
- Its improvements disappear under independent evaluation.

## 31. When to Abandon a Direction

Abandon a research direction when repeated, well-controlled experiments demonstrate that its expected benefits are not supported.

Preserve the negative findings.

Do not keep a mechanism alive simply because considerable development time has been invested in it.

## 32. No Endless Experimentation

Every research program must have:

- A central question.
- A limited set of primary hypotheses.
- Predeclared success criteria.
- A defined deliverable.
- A final decision.
- A clear next step.

Additional experiments are permitted only when they answer a specific unresolved question.

The purpose of experimentation is to build the model, not to generate an endless collection of benchmark reports.

